SGLT2 Inhibitor Induced Osmotic Diuresis
Hydration, Fluid and Volume abnormalities Renal Loss
Increased urinary frequency/volume, Postural dizziness, Genitourinary candidiasis

VISIT CONTEXT

Case ID: HFV-042
Reason for Visit: New-onset severe dizziness, constant urination, and marked lethargy following the initiation of a new diabetes medication, requiring urgent evaluation for severe volume depletion and potential ketoacidosis.
Location: Private residence
Present at Visit: Patient only
Urgency Justification: Telephone triage noted an elderly patient who recently started an SGLT2 inhibitor now experiencing near-syncope upon standing, massive urine output, and severe fatigue. This required an urgent after-hours home assessment to evaluate intravascular volume status and definitively rule out euglycemic diabetic ketoacidosis prior to the next morning's in-hours period.

SUBJECTIVE

Chief Complaint: Constant urination, terrible dizziness when standing, and feeling incredibly weak. Patient states, I have been peeing every hour since starting this new diabetes pill last week, I am constantly thirsty, and today I almost passed out getting out of my chair.
History of Present Illness: The patient reports a recent initiation of an SGLT2 inhibitor (Empagliflozin 10mg) by their endocrinologist one week ago. Over the past five days, there has been a noticeable increase in urinary frequency and volume (polyuria), accompanied by increased thirst (polydipsia) out of proportion to exertion. Today, the patient developed significant postural dizziness and lightheadedness upon standing, resulting in a near-fall. They report a mild, unintentional weight loss of 2kg over the past week. Extreme fatigue and generalized weakness are prominent. Additionally, the patient complains of new, intense localized itching, highly suspicious for genitourinary candidiasis secondary to the induced glycosuria.
Functional Status: Functionally impaired; currently resting in an armchair, afraid to walk due to dizziness.
Social/Safety Concerns: High risk of syncope and subsequent fracture; patient lives alone.

OBJECTIVE

Vitals: BP: 125/80 mmHg (Supine) -> 95/60 mmHg (Standing) | HR: 105 bpm | Temp: 36.8 C | RR: 16 bpm | SpO2: 98% | Wt: 82.0 Kg
General: Appears clinically dehydrated, tired, and mobilizing very cautiously.
Physical Exam: Cardiovascular: Tachycardia is present. A profound orthostatic drop in blood pressure is objectively confirmed, directly reproducing the patient's severe dizziness. Respiratory: Lungs are clear, breathing pattern is normal (no Kussmaul respirations). Genitourinary/Integumentary: The diagnostic triad is clinically evident. There is a confirmed history of increased urinary frequency/volume following medication initiation. Postural dizziness is verified. The patient reports symptoms consistent with genitourinary candidiasis. Examination reveals dry oral mucous membranes and slightly decreased skin turgor, confirming significant volume depletion.
Environmental Observations: Clean, organized living space. The new Empagliflozin medication box is visible on the counter.
Medication Reconciliation: Checked against EHR. Concurrent use of Ramipril (ACE inhibitor), compounding the orthostatic hypotension.

ASSESSMENT

Primary Diagnosis: SGLT2 Inhibitor Induced Osmotic Diuresis - Status: Decompensated volume status
Differentials:

Euglycemic Diabetic Ketoacidosis - Ruled out/unlikely due to: Normal respiratory rate, clear mentation, lack of severe abdominal pain or nausea/vomiting, and a negative point-of-care capillary blood ketone test (0.1 mmol/L). Blood glucose is 8.5 mmol/L.

Urinary Tract Infection - Ruled out/unlikely due to: Absence of fever, dysuria, or suprapubic pain. The localized itching is distinctly candidal in nature rather than bacterial cystitis.

Loop Diuretic Overdose - Ruled out/unlikely due to: Precise medication reconciliation confirming the absence of concurrent loop or thiazide diuretic prescriptions.
Risk Level: Environmental/Social Risk: Moderate

CLINICAL REASONING SUMMARY

The patient presents with severe iatrogenic hypovolemia secondary to osmotic diuresis, a known but occasionally severe consequence of initiating SGLT2 inhibitor therapy. The massive forced glycosuria pulls free water and sodium into the urine, resulting in profound intravascular volume depletion, evidenced by marked orthostatic hypotension and tachycardia. The concurrent use of an ACE inhibitor has likely impaired the patient's compensatory hemodynamic mechanisms. Delaying assessment until standard in-hours would have left the patient at severe risk of a syncopal fall or progressive acute kidney injury. Urgent after-hours assessment confirmed the hemodynamic instability, explicitly ruled out the life-threatening complication of euglycemic DKA via point-of-care testing, mandated temporary cessation of the offending drug, and initiated aggressive oral rehydration. The expected investigation results include prominent glycosuria in the absence of severe hyperglycemia, without significant ketonuria or metabolic acidosis, on routine urinalysis that would assist in confirming the diagnosis formally.

CLINICIAN CARE PLAN

Clinical Interventions: Verified absence of ketosis (ketones 0.1 mmol/L). Instructed patient to withhold the SGLT2 inhibitor (Empagliflozin) immediately until reviewed by their usual GP. Initiated aggressive oral rehydration using a balanced electrolyte solution. Prescribed Clotrimazole 1% cream topically for the genitourinary candidiasis.
Positive Behavioral Change: To correct this dangerous drop in your blood pressure, commit to drinking one full glass of electrolyte fluid immediately after every time you urinate. Keep a written log. When you see the log entries match and notice your dizziness improving when you stand, consciously acknowledge that your deliberate fluid replacement is actively preventing a dangerous fall, rewarding your strict compliance.
Home-Based Services (Optional): None.
DME/Safety (Optional): None.
Follow-up: Urgent GP review within 48 hours to reassess volume status, check renal function (UEC), and determine the safety of re-initiating the SGLT2 inhibitor at a lower dose or with altered concurrent anti-hypertensives.

SAFETY-NETTING ESCALATION PLAN

Proceed to the Emergency Department immediately or call 000 if you experience a complete loss of consciousness, if you develop severe, constant abdominal pain and vomiting, or if you begin breathing very rapidly and deeply.

PATIENT INFORMATION

Disclaimer: This is a significant side effect of your new medication requiring temporary cessation and urgent GP follow-up to protect your kidney function.
Clinical Course: The new medication forces your kidneys to dump sugar into your urine. This sugar acts like a magnet, pulling massive amounts of water out of your blood, causing your blood pressure to crash. Stopping the pill and drinking electrolyte fluids will slowly refill your vascular system.

SAFETY-NETTING ADVICE

